      "subsections": {
        "OVERVIEW": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Electric cord injury is an uncommon event that occurs when an animal bites an electric",
              "pdf_page": 522,
              "printed_page": 440,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "cord.",
              "pdf_page": 522,
              "printed_page": 440,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Other causes of electrocution are uncommon in dogs and cats but can occur.",
              "pdf_page": 522,
              "printed_page": 440,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Household electrical currents are alternating (50–60 Hz), 110–120 or 220–240 volts depending on geographic location, and are",
              "pdf_page": 522,
              "printed_page": 440,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "dangerous.",
              "pdf_page": 522,
              "printed_page": 440,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Injury can be caused due to thermal injury or due to disruption of normal electrophysiologic activity of excitable tissue.",
              "pdf_page": 522,
              "printed_page": 440,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Pulmonary edema can be a sequela to electrocution and the pathophysiology is thought to be neurogenic and centrally mediated, leading to pulmonary hyperten-",
              "pdf_page": 522,
              "printed_page": 440,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "sion.",
              "pdf_page": 522,
              "printed_page": 440,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cataract formation is reported following electrocution.",
              "pdf_page": 522,
              "printed_page": 440,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
